workedExamples.question_text:
The grid has $5$ rows and $3$ columns. Let $A$ be its top-left corner and $B$ its bottom-right corner. Paths from $A$ to $B$ use only down and right moves.

[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"template rectangular-proof-grid"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-40.6) rectangle (24.6,0.6);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-40);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-40);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-8)--(24,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-16)--(24,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-24)--(24,-24);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-32)--(24,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (24,-40);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

By splitting paths at the grid points reached after $3$ moves, prove that $\binom{8}{3}=\sum_{k=0}^{3}\binom{3}{k}\binom{5}{3-k}$.

workedExamples.solution_text:
Each path has $3$ right moves and $5$ down moves. Choosing the positions of the right moves gives $\binom{8}{3}$ paths.

Measure each grid point from $A$ by its number of right and down moves. After $3$ moves, every path reaches exactly one of the points $(0,3),(1,2),(2,1),(3,0)$, where the first coordinate counts right moves.

The crosses mark all four split points. Write the general split point as $C_k=(k,3-k)$; the labelled point illustrates $k=1$. The sections from $A$ to $C_k$ and from $C_k$ to $B$ have $3$ and $5$ moves respectively.

[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"rectangular grid diagonal proof solution"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\path[use as bounding box] (-8,-40.6) rectangle (24.6,8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-40);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-40);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-8)--(24,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-16)--(24,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-24)--(24,-24);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-32)--(24,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (24,-40);
\draw[black,dashed,line width=0.6pt] (0,-24)--(24,0);
\foreach \x/\y in {0/-24,8/-16,16/-8,24/0}{
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (\x-1.2,\y-1.2)--(\x+1.2,\y+1.2);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (\x-1.2,\y+1.2)--(\x+1.2,\y-1.2);
}
\fill[black] (0,0) circle[radius=0.7];
\fill[black] (24,-40) circle[radius=0.7];
\path (0,0) node[above left] {$A$};
\path (24,-40) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (8,-16) node[below right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$C_k$};
\path (32,-10) node[anchor=west] {$A$ to $C_k$: $3$ moves};
\path (32,-24) node[anchor=west] {$C_k$ to $B$: $5$ moves};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

For the point $(k,3-k)$, the first section has $k$ right moves among $3$ moves: $\binom{3}{k}$ arrangements. The remaining section has $3-k$ right moves among $5$ moves: $\binom{5}{3-k}$ arrangements.

Multiply the section counts within each case. The four cases are disjoint and cover every path, so $\binom{8}{3}=\sum_{k=0}^{3}\binom{3}{k}\binom{5}{3-k}$.

workedExamples.question_text:
Paths across this $4\times4$ grid go from the top-left corner $A$ to the bottom-right corner $B$, using only down and right moves.

[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"template square-proof-grid"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-32.6) rectangle (32.6,0.6);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (24,0)--(24,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-8)--(32,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-16)--(32,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-24)--(32,-24);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (32,-32);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Split paths at the diagonal of points reached after $4$ moves to prove that $\binom{8}{4}=\sum_{k=0}^{4}\left(\binom{4}{k}\right)^2$.

workedExamples.solution_text:
Each path has $4$ right moves and $4$ down moves, giving $\binom{8}{4}$ paths in total.

Measure grid points from $A$, with coordinates counting right and down moves. The diagonal reached after $4$ moves consists of $(0,4),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(4,0)$. Every path reaches exactly one of these points.

The crosses mark all five split points. Write the general split point as $C_k=(k,4-k)$; the labelled point illustrates $k=2$. Both sections, from $A$ to $C_k$ and from $C_k$ to $B$, have $4$ moves.

[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"square grid diagonal proof solution"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (24,0)--(24,-32);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-8)--(32,-8);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-16)--(32,-16);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-24)--(32,-24);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (32,-32);
\draw[black,dashed,line width=0.6pt] (0,-32)--(32,0);
\foreach \x/\y in {0/-32,8/-24,16/-16,24/-8,32/0}{
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (\x-1.2,\y-1.2)--(\x+1.2,\y+1.2);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (\x-1.2,\y+1.2)--(\x+1.2,\y-1.2);
}
\fill[black] (0,0) circle[radius=0.7];
\fill[black] (32,-32) circle[radius=0.7];
\path (0,0) node[above left] {$A$};
\path (32,-32) node[below right] {$B$};
\path (16,-16) node[below right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$C_k$};
\path (40,-8) node[anchor=west] {$A$ to $C_k$: $4$ moves};
\path (40,-22) node[anchor=west] {$C_k$ to $B$: $4$ moves};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

At $(k,4-k)$, the first half has $\binom{4}{k}$ arrangements. The second half needs $4-k$ right moves among $4$ moves, giving $\binom{4}{4-k}$ arrangements.

By symmetry, the number of complete paths through this point is $\binom{4}{k}\binom{4}{4-k}=\left(\binom{4}{k}\right)^2$.

Adding the disjoint cases counts every path once. Therefore, $\binom{8}{4}=\sum_{k=0}^{4}\left(\binom{4}{k}\right)^2$.
